{
  "teamId": 137,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 San Francisco Giants were supposed to be a transitional team — a franchise in the quiet years between dynasties, filling out lineups with veterans past their prime and prospects not yet ready. Instead, they became one of the most stunning stories in baseball, winning 107 games and capturing the NL West crown in a season that defied every expectation. From the very first day, they showed a resilience and a depth that would define their summer, scratching back from early deficits and piling up wins in ways no one predicted.\n\nApril and May were a proving ground. The Giants stumbled out of the gate on Opening Day when Tyler Rogers surrendered the lead, but Alex Dickerson answered immediately with a home run that set the tone for the entire season — this team would not stay down. Brandon Crawford, entering what many thought was the twilight of his career, started hitting like an All-Star. Austin Slater delivered clutch long balls. The rotation and bullpen were not perfect — young arms like Gregory Santos and Camilo Doval had their rough moments — but the team kept winning, posting 34 victories in the first two months and establishing themselves as legitimate NL contenders.\n\nJune through August was when the Giants truly separated themselves. Grand slams from Mike Tauchman and Mike Yastrzemski swung massive games. LaMonte Wade Jr. emerged as a late-inning hero, earning the nickname 'Late Night LaMonte' with his clutch hitting. The trade deadline brought Kris Bryant, who slotted in immediately and delivered. August was their best month, a 19-9 record fueled by walk-off moments and dominant pitching, capped by a scorching September run of 21-6 that clinched the division and set a modern franchise record for wins. They had outpaced the rival Dodgers — another 106-win team — wire to wire.\n\nThe postseason brought the cruelest ending imaginable. The Giants drew the Dodgers in the NLDS, and through five games the series was everything baseball could ask for — tight, tense, historic. Buster Posey homered in Game 1, Evan Longoria went deep in Game 3, and Darin Ruf gave the Giants life in a desperate Game 5. But in the end, with the season on the line, Camilo Doval yielded the decisive blow — Cody Bellinger's clutch single plating the go-ahead run — and the greatest Giants regular season in decades ended one step short of the NLCS. The loss stung all the more for how close it was, but it could not erase the joy of a 107-win season that reminded a city what its team was capable of.",
  "chapters": [
